-- =============================================================================
-- A multi-clause DECIDE takes its inputs from the GIVEN above it, one per
-- pattern. When the counts differ, the error says so in clause terms, at the
-- first clause, and names no input the drafter did not write (review of
-- legalese/l4-ide#545). An earlier build reported `_pm_arg_2` as an unknown
-- name at 1:1, and `_pm_arg_1` "at <no location>".
-- =============================================================================
DECLARE Colour IS ONE OF Red, Green, Blue
DECLARE Shape IS ONE OF
  Circle HAS colour IS A Colour
  Square

-- More patterns than the GIVEN names inputs.
GIVEN c IS A Colour
GIVETH A NUMBER
DECIDE `too many patterns` Red   Red IS 1
DECIDE `too many patterns` Green c   IS 2

-- Fewer.
GIVEN c IS A Colour
      d IS A Colour
GIVETH A NUMBER
DECIDE `too few patterns` Red   IS 1
DECIDE `too few patterns` Green IS 2

-- A pattern variable and a GIVEN input with the same name are both in scope
-- in the first clause's body, so `c` there is ambiguous, as it would be in a
-- CONSIDER written by hand. The message gives the GIVEN input's location; an
-- earlier build called it "predefined", and reported a second ambiguity at
-- 1:1 for the generated test of the second input, which is now never
-- ambiguous.
GIVEN s IS A Shape
      c IS A Colour
GIVETH A Colour
DECIDE tint (Circle c) Red IS c
DECIDE tint s          c   IS c

-- A GIVEN that declares only a type names no inputs, so it does not name one
-- per pattern either. An earlier build reported the made-up `input 1` as
-- unknown, twice, at 1:1.
GIVEN a IS A TYPE
GIVETH A NUMBER
DECIDE `type only` Red   IS 1
DECIDE `type only` Green IS 2

-- A clause body that reads an input the GIVEN names, as the type the GIVEN
-- declares, draws no second error. An earlier build reported `c` as unknown.
GIVEN c IS A Colour
GIVETH A Colour
DECIDE `reads the given` Red   Red IS c
DECIDE `reads the given` Green d   IS Blue

-- ... and is checked against the type the GIVEN declares, so a body that
-- uses it as something else is reported as that. An earlier build gave it a
-- type nothing constrained: `c + 1` passed, and the IF below reported `c`
-- as a NUMBER, the type it took from the clause above.
GIVEN c IS A Colour
GIVETH A NUMBER
DECIDE `misreads the given` Red   Red IS c + 1
DECIDE `misreads the given` Green d   IS IF c THEN 1 ELSE 2
